# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE, CYCLE 4 on discourse (plans/01-author.md, re-question).
# Superseded: the cycle-3 12-row declared-link probe, kept at
# scenarios/discourse.yaml.declared-link-12row.bak.
# The number that killed it: baseline `dependents` 7/12 = 0.583, sense 11/12 = 0.917,
# delta +0.333. Recall caps at 1.00, so a baseline at 0.583 capped the delta at +0.417
# before the pair ran.
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   (window = plain arm <= 0.50 AND ours >= +0.50 above it, both at once)
#   cycle 0  two-step probe, thin ask, 15 rows   base 6/15  = 0.400   sense 14/15  +0.533  INSIDE
#   cycle 1  seven-step briefed session, 15 rows base 11/15 = 0.733   sense 14/15  +0.200  BASE TOO HIGH
#   cycle 2  two-step probe, 22 rows (+7 assoc)  base 12/22 = 0.5455  sense 14/22  +0.091  BASE TOO HIGH
#   cycle 3  two-step probe, 12 declared links   base 7/12  = 0.583   sense 11/12  +0.333  BASE TOO HIGH
#
#   I am correcting from the BASELINE-TOO-HIGH side for the third time. Intended landing:
#   baseline at or below 0.25, ours at or above 0.75.
#
# THE KILLER I RAN BEFORE DRAFTING, AND WHAT IT DID TO CYCLE 3's REASONING
#   Cycle 3's gold was cut on a per-row tally ("the baseline missed these 2 of 3 times").
#   The confound killer for that tally is: does the row's take/miss co-vary with anything
#   other than the row? Cross-tabbed cycle 0 against cycle 2 - their two step prompts are
#   BYTE-IDENTICAL (checked in python: steps[0].prompt == and steps[1].prompt ==, both True)
#   and cycle 2's gold is a strict superset of cycle 0's fifteen rows. On those same fifteen
#   rows, with that same question:
#
#     cycle0 n=15 cited=6  ['d:backup-restorer','d:email-receiver','d:tags-search',
#                           'd:topic-guardian','d:topic-view-serializer','d:topics-controller']
#     cycle2 n=22 cited=12 (of the shared 15: 12)
#     flipped miss->take:  ['d:chat-channel-fetcher','d:chat-integration-helper',
#                           'd:search-indexer','d:seed-topics','d:topic-voting',
#                           'd:user-destroyer','d:user-notifications']
#
#   SEVEN of fifteen rows flipped from miss to take with nothing changed but the run. So the
#   plain arm's row-level result on this anchor carries +-7 rows of run-to-run noise at n=1,
#   and cycle 3 built its gold on a 3-run tally inside that noise. Cycle 0's PROCEED at 0.400
#   is the low end of the same distribution, not a property of its rows.
#   CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS DRAFT: a row is not admitted because a tally says the baseline missed
#   it. It is admitted on a structural argument, and the tally is reported beside it, never
#   used as the reason.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES THE NOISE (4 baseline runs each, inverse_frequency.py --by-path, model-scoped)
#     migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb                     baseline 0/4
#     plugins/discourse-rss-polling/.../poll_feed.rb                 baseline 0/4
#   Those are the only two rows never taken across four runs, and both sit outside the tree
#   every measured baseline route swept. Two rows is not a group; it is the seed of one.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL ARGUMENT THIS GOLD IS BUILT ON
#   Every one of the four baseline routes on record chose a SEARCH SCOPE first and then
#   enumerated inside it, and the scope was always some subset of `app lib plugins` with
#   `--include=*.rb`:
#     cycle 0  grep -rln "Category\b" app lib | xargs grep -c "Category\." | sort -rn | head -45
#     cycle 2  grep -rn "Category\." app lib plugins/*/lib plugins/*/app   + a /tmp/encl.rb
#              walking {app,lib}/**/*.rb and plugins/**/*.rb for the owning def
#     cycle 3  grep -rn "belongs_to :category..." app lib plugins  +  "Category\.<method>"
#   None of the four ever searched `script/`, `migrations/`, or a `.rake` file, and the two
#   rows that survived four runs are exactly the ones that sit there. The index does not
#   choose a scope: `sense_blast` returns the whole radius in one call and prints
#   direct_callers_by_area over 44 areas, including `migrations/lib/uploader/tasks`,
#   `script/bulk_import`, `script/import_scripts`, `lib/tasks` and seventeen plugin areas.
#   THAT is the seam this draft aims at: not where the name is dark, but where the sweep has
#   to be aimed. Fourteen rows, fourteen distinct areas, no two under one scope.
#
# THE OTHER LEVER, AND WHY (a sense-side fix, from cycle 2's own numbers)
#   Cycle 2: sense mentioned 20 of 22 and cited only 14. Six rows were reached and named and
#   never pinned, because the arm answered them as one grouped bullet. Step 2 here therefore
#   demands the answer be organised by area with every area accounted for, and says an
#   unfinished area must be declared as unfinished. `direct_callers_by_area` hands that
#   shape to the sense arm and hands nothing to a grep.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Honest answer: it IS an occurrence list. This is kind one, and it is written down as kind
#   one rather than dressed up. What makes it the sort that banked +0.53 rather than the sort
#   that failed five times: the ask arrives with step 1's budget already spent, it demands the
#   OWNING ROUTINE and a per-row consequence rather than a location, and the fourteen rows are
#   scattered across fourteen areas in five separate trees so that no single scope choice
#   returns them. The five cold failures asked for a set that one scope covered.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, three candidates profiled, RANKING ONLY)
#   Category      app/models/category.rb   app callers 89, SCATTER 47 dirs,
#                 grep-noise 109 app files, PRECISION 0.82   <- chosen (best of the three)
#   CategoryUser  app/models/category_user.rb  app callers 29, SCATTER 13, PRECISION 1.21
#   CategoryList  app/models/category_list.rb  app callers  7, SCATTER  7, PRECISION 3.50
#   0.82 sits in the tie zone (a deliberate control tie profiled 0.78; the +1.00 winner
#   profiled 0.078). Precision ranks and never kills, and it is the best available here:
#   the two alternatives are ABOVE 1.00, which means false caller edges, not darkness.
#   Recorded, not worked around.
#
# RING: ring_sweep.py --top 12 returns "0 candidate(s) above the bar" on this clone - every
#   one of the twelve anchors reports rows 0, files 0, vias 0. The "what HOLDS X" kind is off
#   the menu on this stack, as it was on mastodon.
#
# MEMORIZATION: memorization_probe.py Category -> closed-book recall 0.174 (< 0.3),
#   "not recited". The forty recited names are all Category's own members
#   (ensure_slug, find_by_slug_path, clear_subcategory_ids, ...); no gold row here is a member
#   of Category - every one is an external call site in tooling or a bundled plugin.
#
# TOKEN COUNTS, RECORDED AND NOT DECIDING ANYTHING: no file in the shown blast payload is
#   token-dark. The lowest `categor` count over all ~80 payload files is 2. Darkness is not
#   the mechanism here and was not selected for.
#
# THE NAMED RISK OF THIS DRAFT (it fails on the SENSE side, not the baseline side)
#   Sense's own per-path record on the two seed rows is 2/4 each. If the sense arm reports
#   thirty dependents chosen from an 88-file radius and only half of them are these fourteen,
#   it lands near 0.50 and the delta misses even against a baseline at 0.15. The per-area
#   accounting demand in step 2 is the whole mitigation. The next run settles it.
# ============================================================================
name: Discourse work session - audit the category contract before a rework
repo: discourse
contract_symbol: Category
contract_file: app/models/category.rb

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that represents one of this forum's
  top-level containers - the thing topics are filed under, that carries its own
  permissions, settings and hierarchy, and that half the product reaches for whenever it
  needs to know where something belongs or who is allowed to see it. The rework will change
  what that class hands back to the code that asks it questions, and it will change the
  shape of what it stores. Before you touch it you have to know two things: how the rest of
  the system gets hold of one of these when all it has is the human-readable path out of a
  URL, and everywhere else in the codebase that would have to be reviewed or changed with
  it. Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate
  could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not
  count, a set of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of
  them does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with
      the one part of this class's job that every incoming request leans on: getting hold
      of one of these records when all the system has is the human-readable path the
      visitor typed, which may name a parent and a child, may be an old path that has since
      been retired, and may not name one of these at all. Trace that path end to end. Show
      every entry point that turns such a path into one of these records, every place that
      walks the hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another, every place that keeps
      the path in step when the record is renamed, and every place that redirects or
      refuses because the path turned out to point somewhere else. For each one give the
      file:line, the name of the routine it lives in, and one phrase on what it contributes
      to that path. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework would have to preserve for
      that path to keep working. A place named without the routine it lives in does not
      count, and neither does a routine named without the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: Resolution path traced through the routines that make it up, each located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
      first part already cost you. Widen back out from that one path to the class as a
      whole, as a single subject, and find EVERY place in the codebase that would have to
      be reviewed or changed with it, so the rework cannot silently break one. Most of what
      you are looking for is not doing this class's job at all: it is in the middle of some
      other job entirely, it reaches for this class exactly once to settle a question that
      other job needs answered, and the routine it does that from is named after the other
      job and never after this one. So the answer you owe is not where the dependency sits
      but WHOSE behaviour changes: for every dependent, name the routine the dependency is
      inside, give its file:line, and say in one phrase what that routine would start
      getting wrong if the class stopped handing back what it hands back today. Organise
      the answer by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when
      every dependent inside it is listed separately with its own location - if you cannot
      finish an area, say which one and say what you left in it, because an area you
      quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing in it. A file:line with no routine
      named around it does not count, a routine you cannot state the consequence for does
      not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every dependent located at file:line with its own consequence named
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre and its obvious write path. Both arms reach these and
  # ---- they do not decide anything. Carried unchanged from cycle 3.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/category.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category.rb:1083 `def self.find_by_slug_path(slug_path)` - the contract under rework and the class-level entry point every slug-path lookup bottoms out in; :1103 `find_by_slug_path_with_id` is the trailing-id variant that falls back to it at :1110, :985 `def self.query_category(slug_or_id, parent_category_id)` is the one-level lookup the hierarchy walk is built from, :627 `def ensure_slug` keeps the stored slug in step across a rename, and :16 `SLUG_REF_SEPARATOR = \":\"` is the separator the ref form is built from"
  - id: c:categories-controller
    group: contract
    match: [app/controllers/categories_controller.rb]
    relation: "app/controllers/categories_controller.rb:394 `category = Category.find_by_slug_path(params[:slug_path].split(\"/\"))` inside `CategoriesController#find` (def at :387) - the HTTP front door onto the slug-path lookup, raising Discourse::NotFound when it comes back blank"
  - id: c:hierarchical-search
    group: contract
    match: [app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb]
    relation: "app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb:53 `scope = Category.from(\"allowed_categories\").select(:id)` inside `Category::Query::HierarchicalSearch#matched_scope` - walks the parent/child hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another"
  - id: c:permalink
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/permalink.rb]
    relation: "app/models/permalink.rb:82 `return category.relative_url if category` - the retired-path case: an old URL that no longer resolves is redirected through the permalink record's category association declared at line 6"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: fourteen one-touch call sites, one per file, fourteen distinct areas
  # ---- across five trees (app, lib, migrations, script, plugins). Every row is present in
  # ---- the shown sense_blast payload for Category (direct_callers), checked against
  # ---- mcp_probe output before it was written. Every line below was opened and read.

  # -- tree: migrations/ (never searched by any measured baseline route)
  - id: d:migrations-optimizer
    group: dependents
    match: [uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb:43 `@category_id = Category.last.id` inside `Migrations::Uploader::Tasks::Optimizer#initialize_discourse_resources` (def at :40) - the upload optimiser grabs one arbitrary container up front purely to have something to attach its synthetic uploads to; it would start resolving a different record, or nil on an empty install, if the default ordering or the store shape moved under it"
  - id: d:migrations-importer-categories
    group: dependents
    match: [importer/steps/categories/categories.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/importer/steps/categories/categories.rb:124 `@highest_position = Category.unscoped.maximum(:position) || 0` inside `Migrations::Importer::Steps::Categories#execute` (def at :123) - the importer reads the current maximum sort position so freshly imported containers are appended after the existing ones; it would start filing every import at a colliding position if `position` stopped being the ordering column"

  # -- tree: script/ (never searched by any measured baseline route)
  - id: d:bulk-import-merger
    group: dependents
    match: [bulk_import/discourse_merger.rb]
    relation: "script/bulk_import/discourse_merger.rb:230 `existing = Category.where(slug: cat_row[\"slug\"]).or(Category.where(name: cat_row[\"name\"])).first` inside `BulkImport::DiscourseMerger#category_exists` (def at :226) - the merge tool decides whether an incoming container is the same one as an existing one by matching slug or name and then comparing parents at :232; it would start duplicating containers, or silently merging two different ones, if slug uniqueness or the parent link changed"

  # -- tree: lib/tasks (.rake - invisible to every `--include=*.rb` sweep on record)
  - id: d:search-rake
    group: dependents
    match: [tasks/search.rake]
    relation: "lib/tasks/search.rake:32 `Category.find_each do |c|` inside `reindex_search` (def at :7) - the manual reindex task walks every container and re-feeds it to the search indexer; it would start leaving containers out of the search index, with no error, if the class stopped enumerating them the way it does today"

  # -- tree: app/
  - id: d:reindex-search-job
    group: dependents
    match: [scheduled/reindex_search.rb]
    relation: "app/jobs/scheduled/reindex_search.rb:112 `Category` opening the chained query joined to `category_search_data` at :113, inside `Jobs::ReindexSearch#load_problem_category_ids` (def at :111) - the scheduled reindexer finds containers whose search rows are stale by locale or index version; it would start reindexing everything, or nothing, if that join column or the id enumeration moved"
  - id: d:post-revision
    group: dependents
    match: [models/post_revision.rb]
    relation: "app/models/post_revision.rb:36 `@categories ||= Category.with_parents(modifications[\"category_id\"])` inside `PostRevision#categories` (def at :33) - a post revision resolves the before/after container ids stored in its modifications blob, and it needs the PARENT of each one so the revision history can render the full path; it would start rendering an edit's category change with a missing or wrong parent"
  - id: d:post-revision-serializer
    group: dependents
    match: [serializers/post_revision_serializer.rb]
    relation: "app/serializers/post_revision_serializer.rb:320 `Category.secured(scope).find_by(id: category_id)&.id` inside `PostRevisionSerializer#filter_category_id` (def at :318) - the revision payload is scrubbed so a viewer never learns the id of a container they cannot see; it would start leaking a restricted container id, or blanking a visible one, if the security scope stopped filtering the way it does"

  # -- tree: plugins/
  - id: d:rss-polling
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-rss-polling/app/jobs/jobs/discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb:15 `return if @discourse_category_id.present? && !Category.exists?(@discourse_category_id)` inside `Jobs::DiscourseRssPolling::PollFeed#execute` (def at :8) - the feed poller aborts the whole job when the container configured for a feed has gone away; it would start posting imported feed items into a container that no longer exists, or stop polling working feeds, if existence stopped being answerable by bare id"
  - id: d:templates-rake
    group: dependents
    match: [lib/tasks/discourse-templates.rake]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-templates/lib/tasks/discourse-templates.rake:113 `Category.find_by(id: SiteSetting.discourse_templates_categories&.split(\"|\")&.first.to_i)` inside `migrate_data` (def at :100) - the canned-replies migration resolves the configured destination container out of a pipe-separated setting string and raises at :116 when it is missing; it would start migrating every template into a newly created container instead of the configured one"
  - id: d:house-ad
    group: dependents
    match: [ad_plugin/house_ad.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-adplugin/app/models/ad_plugin/house_ad.rb:64 `category_ids = Category.secured(scope).pluck(:id)` inside `AdPlugin::HouseAd.all_for_logged_in_users` (def at :50) - house ads targeted at containers are filtered to the ones this viewer may see before the ad join at :67; it would start showing an ad that reveals the existence of a restricted container, or hiding ads that should show"
  - id: d:event-finder
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_post_event/event_finder.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-calendar/lib/discourse_post_event/event_finder.rb:165 `Category.subcategory_ids(category_id)` inside `DiscoursePostEvent::EventFinder.filter_by_category` (def at :158) - the calendar expands one container into itself plus its descendants when the caller asked to include subcategories, then filters events on that id set at :171; it would start dropping events filed in child containers out of the calendar"
  - id: d:ai-translation-candidates
    group: dependents
    match: [translation/category_candidates.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-ai/lib/translation/category_candidates.rb:13 `Category.where(id: target_category_ids.split(\"|\").map(&:to_i))` inside `DiscourseAi::Translation::CategoryCandidates.get` (def at :10) - the translation backlog is built from the containers named in a pipe-separated setting, falling back to `Category.none` at :15; it would start queueing every container for translation, or none, if that relation stopped being chainable"
  - id: d:slack-migration
    group: dependents
    match: [migrate_from_slack_official.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-chat-integration/app/jobs/onceoff/migrate_from_slack_official.rb:52 `next if !category_id.nil? && !Category.exists?(id: category_id)` inside `Jobs::DiscourseChatMigrateFromSlackOfficial#migrate_data` (def at :35) - the one-off Slack migration skips any stored rule whose container id no longer resolves, where the id was parsed out of a plugin-store key at :48; it would start carrying rules pointed at dead containers into the new integration"
  - id: d:import-scripts-base
    group: dependents
    match: [import_scripts/base.rb]
    relation: "script/import_scripts/base.rb:467 `Category` opening the chained lookup that filters on `parent_category_id` at :468 and a case-folded name at :469, inside `ImportScripts::Base#create_category` (def at :465) - every forum importer decides here whether an incoming container already exists, keyed on parent plus lower-cased name, before falling through to `Category.new` at :486; it would start creating a duplicate container on every re-run of an import, or adopting the wrong existing one, if that identity rule changed"
